# C# Coding Standards

- Prefer explicit `public`, `protected`, or `private` access modifiers on types and members
- Avoid `internal`, `protected internal`, and default (unspecified) access
- Always use explicit `this.` to reference instance members (methods and properties)
- **Primary constructor parameters** are an exception: they are not promoted to instance members. Their scope is closure-like — captured from the constructor and in scope for the entire class body. Reference them by parameter name only (e.g. `userRepository`), never with `this.` (e.g. `this.userRepository` is wrong and can cause build errors)
- Do **not** assign primary constructor parameters to private fields when passing them through unchanged (e.g. `private readonly IUserRepository userRepository = userRepository;` is redundant). Only capture to a field when transforming the value (e.g. `private readonly JwtOptions options = jwtOptions.Value;`)
- Always use braces `{ }` for all control structures, even single-line blocks
- Add line breaks between logical groups of code for readability
- Keep each logical operation visually separate from others
- Never use the `dynamic` type — always use strongly-typed variables, explicit types, or anonymous types
- Always include a space after type cast operators (e.g. `(float) value`, not `(float)value`)
- Use LINQ method syntax (`.Where()`, `.Join()`, `.Select()`, etc.) only; do not use LINQ query syntax (`from`/`join`/`where`/`select`)
- When a method signature, method call, primary constructor parameter list, or other parenthesized argument list spans multiple lines, place the closing `)` on its own line
- For LINQ-to-EF constraints, see `linq-to-ef.mdc`
- For documentation comments, see `documentation-standards.mdc`
